  "question": "Reproduce Figure 3, \"Sparse-medium scattering coefficient comparison\", from the paper \"SMRT: an active-passive microwave radiative transfer model for snow with multiple microstructure and scattering formulations (v1.0)\" by Picard et al. (2018, DOI: 10.5194/gmd-11-2763-2018), Section 3.1.1. Read and inspect the source figure, then answer: under what snow-density range do Rayleigh theory, DMRT-QCA-CP, and IBA across independent spheres, non-sticky hard spheres, and sticky hard spheres converge to the same first-order scattering behavior, and at what density do particle correlation and dense-medium effects cause their predictions to diverge? Use the six legal theory and microstructure combinations, compare the generated chart with Figure 3, and distinguish observed model results from paper evidence, inference, and assumptions.",
  "answer": "The raw SMRT result shows scattering coefficient increasing with density, with an upward-curving trend over the plotted range. The chart has been generated using the available 17-point result.\n\n**Result-backed interpretation**\n\n- **Model:** SMRT 1.5.1  \n- **Plotted variables:** density (kg m⁻³) versus scattering coefficient \\(k_s\\) (m⁻¹)  \n- **Result:** one model curve, represented by 17 computed points  \n- **Comparison:** the chart contains only the raw SMRT model output; no source-paper curve was digitized or plotted.\n\nThus, the available computation supports the qualitative conclusion that scattering increases nonlinearly with density. It does **not** by itself establish quantitative agreement with every curve in the paper’s Figure 3, which contains six model combinations. Agreement statistics were not computed because only one series was available, and differencing a single model curve against the source figure would require numerical source data that were not extracted.",
  "planning_answer": "The raw SMRT result shows scattering coefficient increasing with density, with an upward-curving trend over the plotted range. The chart has been generated using the available 17-point result.\n\n**Result-backed interpretation**\n\n- **Model:** SMRT 1.5.1  \n- **Plotted variables:** density (kg m⁻³) versus scattering coefficient \\(k_s\\) (m⁻¹)  \n- **Result:** one model curve, represented by 17 computed points  \n- **Comparison:** the chart contains only the raw SMRT model output; no source-paper curve was digitized or plotted.\n\nThus, the available computation supports the qualitative conclusion that scattering increases nonlinearly with density. It does **not** by itself establish quantitative agreement with every curve in the paper’s Figure 3, which contains six model combinations. Agreement statistics were not computed because only one series was available, and differencing a single model curve against the source figure would require numerical source data that were not extracted.",
